COMMON PITFALLS
- Just describing (cultural) differences is a superficial application of differences without an analysis of how they might conflict or synergize.
- Showing that diversity leads to higher performance without addressing what specific aspects of differences result in the higher performance.
- Sticking to analysis on an individual level, without explaining how differences alter group dynamics.
- Assigning roles to people based on a superficial understanding of compatibility, without considering alternate approaches like those that bring people out of their comfort zone.

APPLYING THIS HC AT MINERVA
- Business: When trying to understand a business, teamwork is central, and diverse perspectives can be crucial to building a resilient business. However, simply achieving diversity in business is not what #differences is about. Be sure to evaluate what kind of differences are specifically important to consider in a given context and what can help you utilize those differences. For example, you might analyze a company that centers innovation. They require different teamwork structures and composition to bring about innovation, such as diversity in ideas, but also a structure that empowers those naturally able to generate, refine, or crowdsource ideas.
- Science: Within scientific teams, appreciating differences can greatly improve scientific discovery. At various stages of research, it helps to include a variety of perspectives and acknowledge the strengths, weaknesses that different researchers bring to the table. For example, when doing research on a group of gender-queer participants, it would be beneficial to have intersectional perspectives on how those participants would respond to the proposed research, and have background knowledge from different angles (a black trans woman might see other points of improvement than a neurodivergent non-binary person). Differences are also essential to generate creative and groundbreaking hypotheses.
- Civic Projects (and group assignments): Sometimes, doing some team building through understanding each other’s differences can set you up for success in longer projects! It might give clarity on what everyone is good at, so that you know who to count on for what when the time comes. It will also help you understand how people respond differently to any challenging situation that might arise. You can understand how to make teamwork work for different styles of work!
- Law: When studying laws against discrimination it’s important to study the histories and context of discrimination against individuals and groups on the basis of their social position or identities. For example, you might apply #differences when challenging the adequacy of a ruling that found no discrimination present in a case where discrimination appears when multiple identities intersect, as law professor Kimberlé Crenshaw did when she criticized a ruling against five Black women who sued General Motors for discrimination against them as Black women in particular.
- Neurodiversity: Applying this HC can lead to improved educational experiences by recognizing neurodiversity as a dimension of difference. You might come across this when studying theories of learning in psychology courses at Minerva, but it's applicable to educational design more broadly. The #differences HC helps us acknowledge that there is a wide spectrum of neurodiversity. There are natural differences in how students’ brains function, encompassing variations in sensory processing, executive function, motor abilities, and preferred ways of communication. With this in mind, students and educators can follow universal design principles to create environments and practices that benefit all participants, not just specific groups. For educators, there are many examples of good pedagogical practices that support diverse cognitive strengths and preferences. For example, courses with options for autonomy and varying challenge levels benefit all students by acknowledging their unique motivations and capabilities. See this paper for more: Compassionate pedagogy for neurodiversity in higher education. Students can also enhance their own collective learning experiences by moving away from framing 'differences' as deviations from a norm, and instead embracing neurodiversity with compassion and encouraging participation from all students.

APPLYING THIS HC OUTSIDE MINERVA
- Understanding your interactions with others in daily life: Understanding what sets you apart from others can actually help you connect better with them. The first part of this is really understanding how you work yourself , for example by doing a personality test, or from feedback of others (very much related to #selfawareness too!). You will see that you respond differently to certain types of people and events. The second part of understanding interactions comes from understanding what different people exist. Being prepared for different ways that other people think can increase understanding over confusement when interacting with a person you don’t know as well yet. Even within your family, you might find that you have different ways to solve problems, which is totally fine, although it might have led to conflict in the past.
- Amplifying and including perspectives of others: By being aware of the possible differences that exist in our societies, we can recognize at what points – in our work, or daily life – certain perspectives can be beneficial or important to consider. Educating oneself on the different experiences of people can help us empathize and understand how we might get something out of hearing a perspective that hasn’t been infused yet in what we are about to do or think.

